body:
A typical use case is:
- when there're no search results, I want to display a specific component
- when there's no query I want to display a completely different homepage (jadopado like)

Currently in `react-instantsearch` we have a <NoResults/> component that allows us to display specific behavior when there're no results. 

The <NoResults/> current implementation is simple: it returns the child if there're results, and `null` if not. 

It means we can write:

``` jsx
<NoResults>
    <div> No results</div>
<NoResults/>
<div>
//rest of the page
</div>
```

Now the issue is that `// rest of the page` will still be displayed when there're no results: **we need to have an if/else behavior**. 

With the current implementation, we could use an HOC and write something like: 

``` js
function MyResults(props) {
  if (props.noResults) {
    return (
      <div className="results-wrapper">
        <div className="no-results">
          No results found matching <span className="query">{props.query}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    );
  } else {
    return (
      <div className="results-wrapper">
      </div>
    );
  }
}

const ConnectedResults = NoResults.connect(MyResults);
```

As you can see, having a NoResults component is not that useful because the only thing we need is the ability to use the connect function. Also, it's not very simple in a user point of view, regarding that it's a common pattern. 

Now, what we could also do is to give the opportunity to the user to write directly something like this : 

``` jsx
<NoResults>
        <div className="no-results">
          No results found matching <span className="query">{this.props.query}</span>
        </div>
<NoResults/>
<HasResults>
    <stuff>
<HasResults/>
```

And if we want to access parent props from the child, we can change the <NoResults/> implementation to this : 

``` js
render() {
    if (!this.props.noResults) {
      return null;
    }

    return Children.map(this.props.children, child => {
      return React.cloneElement(child, this.props); //perform a merge between parent and child props
    })[0];
  }
```

That's one way to have a simpler api. 

With that in mind, opening the conservation: 
- Are HOC sufficient? Or are those patterns (NoQuery, NoResults, HasQuery ..) too frequent in UIs?
- Is the pattern with NoResults/HasResults is a better way to go (with some more documentation to know the props that are available) 
- Does you have any other ideas about the api we should use?
